# Issue 03 - analysis and actions

Status: resolved

## Request

- Drop the "smart shuffle" (freshness weighting).
- "Random order" stays, without "smart" in the name, but weights the last 100
  shown photos instead of the file date: weight 0.01 for the photo shown last,
  0.02 for the one before, up to 0.99 for the 99th, weight 1 for everything
  never shown. A split pair enters the list as one rank, both photos.
- Three more orders: by EXIF time (fall back to the file creation time), by
  file creation time, by file modification time.

## What was built

- `RandomPlaylistStrategy` now draws against the view history: every photo
  never shown has the weight 1, a photo shown recently is weighted by its rank
  divided by 100 (`rememberedPhotos`), so the photo shown last gets 0.01 and
  the 100th 1.00. The history holds the photos shown together in split mode as
  one rank, keyed by path so a rescan keeps it.
- `SortedPlaylistStrategy` walks the collection oldest first and starts over at
  the end. `PhotoOrderField` holds the three date fields and their fallback
  chain: `exif` -> capture date, then file creation date, then file date;
  `creation` -> file creation date, then file date; `modification` -> file
  date. Photos sharing a date keep a stable order by path, and the walk
  continues from the photo on screen when the order is rebuilt.
- `PlaylistStrategy` gained `recordShown(List<PhotoEntry>)` with an empty
  default; `PhotoService.noteDisplayed(photo, [partner])` forwards it and the
  slideshow calls it after a transition and after a partner refit, which is
  the only place that knows about the photo sharing the frame.
- `PhotoEntry.createdAt` is the new optional file creation date, set from
  `FileStat.changed` by the file system scanner. MediaStore does not report
  one, so the `creation` order walks by file date there.
- `JsonConfigService.photoOrder` only accepts the ids of `PlaylistStrategies.orders`
  and falls back to `random`, so a config still holding `weighted_freshness`
  does not reach the dropdown with no matching entry.
- `ExifMetadataProvider` keeps its results in a cache file in the application
  cache directory, keyed by path and modification date, written after a short
  delay. A file that changed is read again, a corrupted cache is ignored.
- Labels: `photoOrderSmart` removed, `photoOrderExif`, `photoOrderCreation`,
  `photoOrderModification` added (en, de). The order names used by the
  strategies themselves stay English, as before.

## Verified

Automated (`flutter analyze` + `flutter test`, CI run 37025164051, 104 tests):

- `random_playlist_strategy_test.dart`: the photo shown last gets far below the
  weight of an unseen photo, the weight grows with the age of the slide, both
  photos of a split frame share a rank, the history survives a rescan, only
  the last 100 slides keep a weight, every photo of a collection with two
  batches of file dates is reached.
- `sorted_playlist_strategy_test.dart`: the three fields, the fallback chains,
  the wrap at the end of the collection, the rebuild that continues from the
  photo on screen, the stable order of photos sharing a date.
- `exif_metadata_provider_cache_test.dart`: the capture date of a JPEG with an
  EXIF date is read, written to the cache, served from the cache file to a
  provider that starts later even when the file content changed, read again
  once the modification date moved, and a corrupted cache file is ignored.

On the device (POCO F1, `2f64868c`, dev-0103 = `93443bd`):

- MediaStore source, kiosk01 album with 16 photos in two batches (9 synced on
  2026-10-01, 7 keeping their June file dates): `modification` walked all 16
  photos in ascending file date, four full cycles, no out of order step;
  `exif` walked all 16 photos in ascending capture date, two full cycles, no
  out of order step, and in the opposite block order to `modification`; the
  `creation` order walked by file date there, as expected since MediaStore
  reports no creation date.
- Local folder source, six test photos with the modification dates
  L1 < L2 < L3 < P1 < P2 < P3 and the creation dates P3 < P2 < P1 < L3 < L2 <
  L1: `modification` walked L1, L2, L3, P1, P2, P3 and `creation` walked P3,
  P2, P1, L3, L2, L1, each repeating over the whole run. The two orders are
  therefore told apart by the inode change date, not the modification date.
- `random` over the kiosk01 album, 56 slides: the June batch was picked 24
  times (42.9 %), the recent batch 32 times (57.1 %), all 16 photos shown, at
  least 2 picks each. Before the change the same collection gave the June
  batch 4 % of the picks and reached 11 of the 16 photos (issues/01).
- Settings: the `Photo Order` row shows `Random order` and its dropdown offers
  exactly `Random order`, `By capture time (EXIF)`, `By file creation time`,
  `By file modification time`.

## Notes

- The dates are walked ascending, oldest photo first, which is the literal
  reading of "by exif time". Flipping the direction is one line in
  `SortedPlaylistStrategy` if newest first is wanted.
- The EXIF order improves as photos are displayed: a photo that was never shown
  is ordered by its file date until the frame has read its EXIF once. The
  cache keeps that read to one per file and per change.
- The 100th remembered photo gets the weight 1.00, which is the weight of a
  photo that was never shown; the issue lists the weights up to 0.99 for the
  99th and leaves that one open.

Reliability: 95 % (every statement above comes from a test run or a device
measurement of dev-0103).